{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Cloud ADF codec — make the existing ADF encode Markdown-aware (bidirectional)\n\n## Context / Problem\nJira Cloud stores rich text as ADF (structured JSON). The reconciler **already\nemits ADF** on the Cloud outbound path (live encode at\n`adapters/jira/acli.py:205` and `acli_cli_ops.py:369`, both calling\n`_text_to_adf` = `adf.text_to_adf`) — it does not push a plain-Markdown string.\nThe `RichTextCodec` seam (epic `e369`, story J3 / `aac8`) routes Cloud rich text\nthrough `AdfCodec` (`adapters/jira/rich_text_codec.py`), a pass-through wrapper\nover the pinned `adapters/jira/adf.py`.\n\nThe real gap: `adf.py`'s encode is **plain-text-structured, not Markdown-aware**.\n`text_to_adf` (`adf.py:275`) splits the body into blocks on blank lines and emits\neach as one `paragraph` node, so Markdown block syntax (`##`, `-`/`1.`, ```` ``` ````)\nand inline syntax (`**bold**`, `~~strike~~`, `[t](url)`) is carried as **literal\ncharacters inside paragraph text nodes** and Cloud renders raw source. Conversely\n`adf_to_text` (`adf.py:26`) already emits **Markdown-style** syntax on decode\n(`**` strong, `*` em, `#` headings, inline code, `|` tables) — so the decode half\nis largely Markdown already; the missing half is a Markdown-aware **encode**.\n\nThis story makes encode+decode Markdown-aware **behind the existing `AdfCodec`\nseam**, adding no new plumbing, so Cloud renders native rich text and a fetched\nADF body decodes back to the same Markdown — truly bidirectional.\n\n## Prior art in this repo (the seam we build on — NOT greenfield)\n- `RichTextCodec` Protocol — `adapters/jira_family/rich_text.py` (four ops).\n- `AdfCodec` (Cloud) — `adapters/jira/rich_text_codec.py`; delegates each op to\n  `adf.py`. Pinned as a wrapper (not a copy) by\n  `test_rich_text_codec.py:154` (`test_adf_codec_is_a_pass_through_to_the_pinned_adf_module`,\n  asserting `to_wire == adf.text_to_adf`, `decode_inbound == adf.adf_to_text(...)`\n  at `:163`/`:164`).\n- `adf.py` — `text_to_adf` (`:275`), `adf_to_text` (`:26`, already Markdown-ish),\n  `normalize_description` (`:310` = `adf_to_text(text_to_adf(text))`),\n  `fit_text_to_adf_limit` (`:341`).\n- `OutboundFieldMapper` — `adapters/jira_family/outbound_mapper.py:77` composes\n  `normalize_outbound(fit_outbound(value))` for the description.\n\n## Engine decision (no fork): vendored `marklas`\nUse **`marklas`** (pure-Python, mistune-based, MIT; `github.com/byExist/marklas`)\nfor Markdown↔ADF, **vendored under `src/rebar/_vendor/marklas/` with the upstream\nversion+sha and its MIT LICENSE recorded** (single-maintainer bus-factor mitigated\nby vendoring). This is a committed decision, not a spike: a direct head-to-head\nagainst `adf.py` over the **live store corpus (5,730 bodies, `rebar export`,\n2026-08-03)** measured marklas at **99.65% idempotent round-trip** (echo-safe\nunder the reconciler's normalize-both-sides model) while producing\n**structurally-rich ADF for 56.7%** of bodies (56.3% both rich AND idempotent).\nThe ≥99.6% AC below locks that in as a regression bar.\n\n### Rejected alternative — extend `adf.py`'s handlers in-house\nWeighed and **rejected on empirical grounds.** The appeal was that `adf.py`'s\n*decode* (`adf_to_text`) already emits Markdown-ish text, suggesting a symmetric\n*encode* is a small change. The live-corpus head-to-head refutes this for the\nhalf that matters — the **encode**:\n- `adf.py`'s `text_to_adf` produces **0%** structurally-rich ADF over the corpus:\n  it flattens **every** construct to paragraph text and emits **zero** ADF marks.\n  Concretely, of the bodies marklas renders as structured ADF, `adf.py` renders\n  **0**: headings (1,924 bodies), bullet lists (2,538), task lists (1,780),\n  ordered lists (795), code blocks (280), tables (60), blockquotes (33), rules\n  (72); inline marks strong (1,396), inline-code (2,569), em (439), strike (7),\n  link (12) — all 0 for `adf.py`.\n- `adf.py`'s 100% round-trip fidelity is therefore **trivial**: it translates no\n  rich structure, so there is nothing to lose. It is a plain-text→paragraph\n  encoder, not a Markdown↔ADF codec.\n- Reaching marklas parity in-house means building a **full GFM→ADF parser +\n  faithful ADF→GFM serializer** — i.e. re-implementing marklas/mistune. The prior\n  hand-rolled mistune attempt already stalled at **90.7%** fixed point, and the\n  residual tail is exactly the emphasis/mark serialization a bespoke engine gets\n  wrong. Only the *decode* half of `adf.py` overlaps; the *encode* is greenfield.\n\n(Experiment scripts + raw counts: session log `5eb0-52a3-45f0-43e5`;\n`files/parity_*.py`, `files/parity-results.md`.)\n\n## Approach\n1. **Add Markdown-aware `markdown_to_adf`/`adf_to_markdown` in `adf.py`** (thin\n   wrappers over vendored marklas), keeping `AdfCodec` a pass-through: repoint\n   `AdfCodec.to_wire`→`markdown_to_adf`, `decode_inbound`→`adf_to_markdown`,\n   `normalize_outbound`→`adf_to_markdown(markdown_to_adf(text))`, and\n   `fit_outbound`→a new `fit_markdown_to_adf_limit` that measures the\n   `markdown_to_adf` serialization (the OLD `fit_text_to_adf_limit` measured the\n   plain encode, so it must be repointed or it no longer bounds the real wire).\n2. **Codec-law invariant.** The four ops must satisfy the pinned law\n   `normalize_outbound(t) == decode_inbound(to_wire(t))` (parametrized in\n   `test_rich_text_codec.py`). The mark-order canonicalizer (below) is applied\n   **identically** inside `to_wire`'s decode-preview, `decode_inbound`, and\n   `normalize_outbound` so the law holds for Markdown fixtures too.\n3. **Loop prevention — reuse the existing convergence.** Keep `normalize_outbound`\n   as the idempotent \"reads-after-round-trip\" transform applied to both sides\n   before the description diff. Fix the one true 2-cycle oscillator — co-located\n   `strong`+`strikethrough` (`**~~x~~**` ⇄ `~~**x~~**`, no canonical mark order) —\n   with a deterministic mark-order canonicalizer inside the codec. The ~18\n   transient whitespace canonicalizations converge under normalize-both-sides.\n4. **Failure path = WHOLE-CODEC degrade (preserves the law).** On any conversion\n   error for a field, degrade THAT field's codec to the plain-text path\n   *consistently* — `to_wire`→`adf.text_to_adf`, `decode_inbound`→`adf.adf_to_text`,\n   `normalize_outbound`→`adf.normalize_description`, `fit_outbound`→\n   `fit_text_to_adf_limit` together (never a mixed Markdown/plain codec, which\n   would break the codec law) — log the body id, and continue the reconcile.\n\n## Convergence-key consumers (blast radius — all preserved)\nMaking `normalize_description`/the encode Markdown-aware changes the description\nCOMPARE keys at two tuned sites; both are in Scope and their semantics preserved:\n- `adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py:564` — normalizes the LOCAL value only and\n  deliberately re-emits each stale body **exactly once** (the plain→rich cutover\n  rides this exact mechanism: every already-synced description re-emits once to\n  upgrade to rich ADF, then converges).\n- `inbound_differ.py:210` — normalizes BOTH sides for the comparison copy only.\n- **Shared plain-text surface UNCHANGED:** `inbound_fields.normalize_rich_text`\n  (ticket 21ca) stays plain-text so `inbound_translate._normalize_adf_body` and\n  `outbound_comments._normalize_comment_body` are unaffected. The description\n  diff's REMOTE-side decode uses the codec's Markdown-aware `decode_inbound`, not\n  the shared plain normalizer.\n- **Comments have no body-diff key:** comment identity is append-only by Jira\n  comment ID (story `789c`), so Cloud comment bodies render via `AdfCodec.to_wire`\n  on CREATE only — there is no comment-body convergence key to make asymmetric. A\n  comment-body convergence test guards this.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/adf.py` — add\n  `markdown_to_adf`/`adf_to_markdown`/`fit_markdown_to_adf_limit`; keep plain\n  `text_to_adf`/`adf_to_text` for the fallback + shared callers.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/rich_text_codec.py` — repoint\n  `AdfCodec` ops + mark-order canonicalizer; whole-codec fallback.\n- `src/rebar/_vendor/marklas/` — vendored, sha-pinned copy.\n- `pyproject.toml` — record the vendored sha.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py` (`:564`)\n  and `inbound_differ.py` (`:210`) — verify the Markdown-aware normalize preserves\n  the once-only re-emit / both-sides-compare semantics.\n- **Guard:** `inbound_fields.normalize_rich_text` NOT changed.\n- `tests/` incl. `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_rich_text_codec.py` — update\n  the pin test to `to_wire == markdown_to_adf` / `decode_inbound == adf_to_markdown`.\n\n## Testing\n- **Pin test (updated):** `test_adf_codec_is_a_pass_through_to_the_pinned_adf_module`\n  asserts `to_wire == adf.markdown_to_adf` and `decode_inbound == adf.adf_to_markdown`\n  (wrapper-not-a-copy preserved).\n- **Codec law:** `normalize_outbound(t) == decode_inbound(to_wire(t))` holds over\n  Markdown fixtures (headings/lists/code-fence/combined-marks).\n- **Structured round trip:** heading+checklist+inline-code+link round-trips\n  MD→ADF→MD to a fixed point, asserting ADF node types (`test_cloud_adf_roundtrip`).\n- **Oscillation:** the two known combined strong+strikethrough bodies converge in\n  ≤1 pass; a permutation property test asserts 100% convergence.\n- **Corpus idempotence:** the round-trip is idempotent (normalize-both-sides\n  stable, `r(r(x))==r(x)`) for ≥99.6% of the 5,730 live-store bodies\n  (`test_cloud_corpus_idempotence`); measured 99.65%.\n- **Whole-codec fallback:** an un-convertible body degrades the WHOLE codec for\n  that field to plain-text and the codec law still holds; the pass does not abort\n  (`test_cloud_adf_conversion_fallback`).\n- **Comment convergence:** a Cloud comment body created via `to_wire` and refetched\n  does not re-sync (`test_cloud_comment_body_convergence`).\n- **Live cutover:** a converted ADF description posted to a live/staging Cloud\n  issue renders as rich text, asserted against the J1 oracle (`acb2`) —\n  `test_cloud_adf_live`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `marklas` is vendored under `src/rebar/_vendor/marklas/` with the upstream\n  sha in `pyproject.toml`; verified by an import test asserting the pinned sha.\n- [ ] `AdfCodec.to_wire` emits Markdown-aware ADF (heading/list/code/emphasis\n  nodes, not literal `#`/`-`/`**`) and `decode_inbound` returns the source\n  Markdown; verified by `test_cloud_adf_roundtrip` asserting node types and the\n  decoded equality.\n- [ ] The pin test asserts `AdfCodec` still delegates to `adf.py`\n  (`to_wire == markdown_to_adf`, `decode_inbound == adf_to_markdown`); verified by\n  the updated `test_adf_codec_is_a_pass_through_to_the_pinned_adf_module`.\n- [ ] The codec law `normalize_outbound(t) == decode_inbound(to_wire(t))` holds\n  over Markdown fixtures; verified by the parametrized law test.\n- [ ] `AdfCodec.fit_outbound` measures the `markdown_to_adf` serialization;\n  verified by `test_adf_fit_measures_the_serialized_document_not_the_plain_text`\n  updated to the Markdown encode.\n- [ ] The combined strong+strikethrough oscillation is eliminated by a\n  mark-order canonicalizer applied identically in all three ops; verified by the\n  regression + permutation property test (100% convergence).\n- [ ] The MD→ADF→MD round-trip is idempotent (normalize-both-sides stable) for\n  ≥99.6% of the 5,730 live-store bodies; verified by `test_cloud_corpus_idempotence`.\n- [ ] A conversion failure degrades the WHOLE codec for that field to plain-text\n  (law preserved) without aborting the reconcile; verified by\n  `test_cloud_adf_conversion_fallback`.\n- [ ] The shared `inbound_fields.normalize_rich_text` still returns plain text;\n  verified by its existing tests staying green.\n- [ ] A converted ADF description posted to a live/staging Cloud issue renders as\n  rich text; verified by `test_cloud_adf_live` against the J1 oracle."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785807145464684001,"uuid":"f87f7340-17fc-46d3-a24b-84b80e1d2228"}